    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Tossing a coin 100 times"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Toss a coin 100 times and you may get 52 heads - close to half. Probability is about how likely things are, and it can be worked out in two ways: by reasoning and by experiment."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "sample space",
          "classical",
          "empirical",
          "0 to 1",
          "die examples",
          "many trials",
          "cards"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "An experiment with several possible outcomes is a trial; the set of all outcomes is the sample space.\nClassical probability: P(E) = number of favourable outcomes / total number of equally likely outcomes.\nEmpirical probability: P(E) = number of times E occurred / total number of trials.\nProbability is always between 0 and 1, with 0 for an impossible and 1 for a sure event.\nFor one die, P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2 and P(prime) = 3/6 since 2, 3 and 5 are prime.\nThe more trials we perform, the closer the empirical probability comes to the classical probability.\nA card drawn from 52: P(king) = 4/52 = 1/13."
        }
      },
